# Line endings that matter to a program, not just to a diff.
#
# Start.bat is the launcher a Windows customer double-clicks. It shipped with
# pure LF endings, which cmd.exe parses inconsistently — parenthesised blocks
# and `for /f` (both of which that file is built from) are where it shows up,
# and the failure mode is an unhelpful "The syntax of the command is incorrect"
# on the very first thing anyone runs. Force CRLF in the working tree and in
# the archive, on every platform, regardless of the developer's core.autocrlf.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# ...and the counterpart: a shell script or a systemd unit with CRLF endings
# fails on Linux with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M".
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
Start.sh text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.bin binary
*.step binary
*.stp binary
